      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm prepping a demo for my Year 10 chemistry class on the volatile compounds in Allium vegetables. Could you give me a detailed breakdown of the chemical pathway that occurs when an onion is sliced, specifically explaining how the enzymatic reaction leads to the production of propanethial S-oxide and why that particular molecule causes the stinging sensation in the eyes?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sure, let's break down the chemistry behind the compound you mentioned. When you cut an onion, it ruptures cell walls, releasing enzymes called alliinase in the presence of water. These enzymes convert sulfenic acid into the gaseous compound called propanethial S-oxide through a series of chemical reactions (mechanism of transsulfenation). Propanethial S-oxide is then released into the air, and when it comes into contact with your eyes, it discharges sulfur dioxide gas, which irritates the eyes, causing the stinging sensation. This stinging sensation is mainly due to the irritant effect of sulfur dioxide on the ocular surface (eczematous reaction).",
