    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The rapid breakdown of sulfoxides within an onion cell upon slicing is an impressive biochemical reaction, befitting the cell's defensive response. The enzymes responsible, such as alliinase and sulthiodiazoles, are primed within their protein complexes called isoenzymes. When the cell membrane is compromised during cutting, these enzymes are released. As they come into contact with their substrates, sulfoxides like allicin—an amino acid derivative—are converted into propanethial S-oxide almost immediately due to the catalytic efficiency of alliinase. This highly efficient enzymatic reaction is due to a lower activation energy barrier and transition state stabilization, allowing the process to occur rapidly and efficiently. The inherent readiness of these enzymes within the cell juxtaposes the suddenness of their activation upon cell damage and the swift conversion of sulfoxides to irritants.",
